## 🎯 Tier 2 — Strong Additions

### 6. **Loyalty Program**
- Points per cedi spent
- Tiered discounts (Bronze / Silver / Gold)
- Redeem points at POS via phone number
- Birthday discounts
- Owner dashboard shows "Top 20 customers this month"

### 7. **Multi-Branch Support**
Currently one tenant = one pharmacy. Real owners run 3–10 branches.
- Branches under one owner account
- Consolidated reporting across branches
- Stock transfers between branches
- Per-branch cashier / pharmacist assignment
- Owner picks which branch to log into

### 8. **Purchase Orders & Supplier Orders**
Bridges the gap between "supplier exists" and "stock arrives":
- Create PO → send to supplier (email/WhatsApp/PDF)
- Track PO status: draft / sent / partial / received
- Auto-update stock on goods-received
- Supplier performance: on-time delivery %, quality

### 9. **Stock Take / Physical Count Mode**
- Snap a "count sheet" for a category
- Cashier/pharmacist enters physical counts
- System shows variance vs expected
- Approval flow: pharmacist submits → owner approves
- Auto-adjust stock with audit trail

**Why:** Pharmacies do this monthly. Today it's paper-based. Huge time saver.

### 10. **Prescription Scanning / OCR**
Currently prescriptions are text entries. Add:
- Upload photo of handwritten prescription
- OCR extracts doctor name, drugs, dosages
- Pharmacist verifies and converts to sale
- Attach image to the prescription record

**Why:** Ghanaian prescriptions are mostly handwritten. This is a real pain point.

### 11. **Offline Mode for POS**
Ghana internet is unreliable. POS must work offline.
- Service worker caches products
- Sales queue locally, sync when online
- Conflict resolution (stock adjustments)
- Offline receipt printing still works

**Why:** If a cashier can't sell when internet drops, they'll abandon the system.

### 12. **Drug Database Auto-Population**
When adding a drug, autocomplete from:
- Ghana FDA drug registry
- WHO essential medicines
- Common Ghanaian brand names (e.g., "Kina", "Cofsils")

Auto-fill: dosage form, standard pack sizes, typical category.

**Why:** Speeds up onboarding dramatically — pharmacist types "para" → 3 options with correct details.

